~ JudithDayTwo
set Player.canMove = false
set NPC.tempElapTime = NPC.elapsedTime
set NPC.tempCycProg = NPC.cycleProgress

Judith: "Oh Mort I haven't seen you in a day but it feels like a lifetime! What's on your mind?"
Is it really time to end Judith's long life?
- Kill Her [if IM.inventory[4] && IM.inventory[5]]
	Now's as good a time as any. Honestly, I'm kind of doing her a favor. Her death will help bring back someone with much more ahead of them.
	Mort: "Ah, Judith! I've been looking everywhere for you!"
	I raised my hands in mock joy as she looked towards me with a soft smile.
	Judith: "Oh my dear Mort, you almost never greet me so warmly! Now, what's the catch?"
	Judith looked at me, feigning disbelief. She wouldn't harm a fly and couldn't fathom it'd be any different for anyone else.
	Mort: "I'm just looking for Ryan, I think he's sick so I'm going to bring him over to my house when I find him. Would you mind waiting inside and whipping up something to help him get better?"
	Judith's heart just about shatters when she hears her dear Ryan is under the weather. There was never a doubt in my mind it'd be easy to convince her to come over.
	Judith: "You don't need to say another word, Mister. I hope you have something good in your fridge."
	Judith: "That boy Chris might be a tried and true chef, but I make food that heals the soul!"
	Mort: "Thank you for lending me a hand, Judith. I really couldn't ask for anything more."
	
	set NPC.canBeKilled = true
	set NPC.elapsedTime = 0
	set NPC.cycleProgress = 0
	set NPC.currentLocation = NPC.get_parent().position
	set NPC.isIdle = false
	
	I barely even got the words out of my mouth before Judith was shuffling her way towards my house.
- Don't Kill Her
	Not everything's ready just yet. I need a little bit more time to gather what I need before I take Judith out of the picture.
	Mort: "Oh nothing really, Judith. Pay me no mind, I was just walking around, getting some fresh air. You know the drill. It okay if I come back around later?"
	Judith: "You know I always love having you around, Mort. Take your time, dear."
	I almost find Judith sympathetic, but she just ends up seeming pathetic in the end.
	set NPC.conversationsHad -= 1

	set NPC.elapsedTime = NPC.tempElapTime
	set NPC.cycleProgress = NPC.tempCycProg
	set NPC.isIdle = false
	set NPC.canMove = true
	set Player.canMove = true

=> END

~ ChrisDayTwo
set Player.canMove = false
set NPC.tempElapTime = NPC.elapsedTime
set NPC.tempCycProg = NPC.cycleProgress

Chris seems a bit off-kilter as I walk by. I know he's off his game because he almost doesn't call me over.
Almost.
Chris: "Mr. Mort! How's it hanging?"
I don't think of myself as very old, I'm only 28, but god does Chris grow my distaste for "the youth".
Mort: "Well hello there, Chris. Got anything fun and interesting planned for today like always?"
Chris shakes his head in disappointment.
Chris: "No sir, Mr. Mort. I'm just about out of food in my cupboards! Do you mind if I come over tomorrow to borrow a few things before I can make it to the city and get groceries?"
This was almost too perfect. He was already going to be next on my list, but I didn't even have to ask him over.
Mort: "Of course, Chris. I'll let you know when the perfect time to come on over is. Is that all that's getting you down? I'd kill to have problems like that be my biggest worry."
Chris's face light up in embarassment. He might have lofty ambitions, but he's still just a kid at heart.
Chris: "Oh I'm sorry, Mr. mort. I didn't mean to whine about silly stuff like that, that's my bad. I'll make something for you soon to make up for it, alright?"
I didn't even have to force a smile this time. I was genuinely excited to see Chris again the next day.
Mort: "You really don't have to, Chris. You already do more than enough for us in the village. Harry thought so too."
It sickened me to bring up Harry to Chris, but it seemed to lift his spirits. Good, the less that seemed wrong in the village, the better.
Mort: "I'll see you tomorrow, Chris."
Chris: "See you tomorrow, Mr. Mort!"
Like a golden retriever, he was back to his usual high energy in a near instant. I couldn't get away fast enough.

set NPC.elapsedTime = NPC.tempElapTime
set NPC.cycleProgress = NPC.tempCycProg
set NPC.isIdle = false
set NPC.canMove = true
set Player.canMove = true

=> END

~ AgathaDayTwo
set Player.canMove = false
set NPC.tempElapTime = NPC.elapsedTime
set NPC.tempCycProg = NPC.cycleProgress

Agatha: "Mortimer."
Mort: "Agatha."
It was always the same with Agatha, but that sense of sameness meant she was always attentive to small differences. I had to throw her off.
Mort: "Agatha, I wanted to tell you that I really appreciate everything you've done for Harry and I over the years. You've truly been a blessing."
That sort of candid show of affection really threw Agatha off. Everything was usually so to-the-point with us. It didn't help that neither of us were used to Harry being mentioned.
Agatha: "Well I... Uh..."
She coughed. It was almost fun to see her so out of sorts. However, she quickly gathered herself.
Agatha: "You know this village and everyone in it mean more than anything to me."
She was fighting back tears, probably thinking of Harry. That hypocrite.
Mort: "Oh, trust me Agatha, I'm almost painfully aware of your watchful eye."
Mort: "Speaking of, I think I saw Ryan head off to the city. Do you know what for?"
Agatha looks even more caught off guard than before, almost like her pride was hurt.
Agatha: "No I... I didn't realize... That's strange, he usually would have told me."
I shook my head, feigning confusion. As long as she didn't realize he was dead, that was the goal.
Mort: "Seemed like a tool of his broke. Must've been something he couldn't have fixed himself."
Agatha nodded her head. She wasn't fully content with it, but it was good enough for now.
Agatha: "Well, if that's the case, he should be back soon. I trust him to know those city folk aren't the safest to be around for long."
She pauses for a moment before looking back at me, having been lost in thought.
Agatha: "Not you, though. We've all loved you since Harry brought you back here. I know I say it all the time, but you're family to us, you know?"
Ugh, there she went again. She was always harping on about family. I was sick of it.
Mort: "I really appreciate it Agatha. Say, I'll look out for Mort myself, you take some time off, you seem a bit stressed."
Agatha couldn't really disagree with that sentiment with the state she was in. She nodded as the two of us parted ways for the day.

